WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.881 --> 00:00:13.021
to Geneva, Switzerland!

2
00:00:13.882 --> 00:00:19.245
I am Jean-Paul Baptiste, a watchmaker whose family moved here from France about a century ago.

3
00:00:20.185 --> 00:00:25.789
My grandfather, who was a watchmaker too, used to say to me, "Jean-Paul, all the best watchmakers are

4
00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:34.469
French." But now, in 1676, many French watchmakers live in Geneva; in fact, Geneva has the most watchmakers

5
00:00:34.509 --> 00:00:35.129
in the world.

6
00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:41.511
We are called cabinotiers because we work in shops almost as small as a cabinet.

7
00:00:42.671 --> 00:00:47.472
But even though our workshops may be small, the workmanship of our watches is grand indeed.

8
00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:54.114
Becoming a watchmaker takes hard work and patience because the parts of a watch are so tiny.

9
00:00:55.194 --> 00:01:00.596
The precision needed to create or repair a watch takes a long time to learn, so you must

10
00:01:00.636 --> 00:01:01.716
start as an apprentice.

11
00:01:03.192 --> 00:01:08.775
For example, when I was 15, I apprenticed to an excellent watchmaker who is a friend of my

12
00:01:08.815 --> 00:01:15.939
grandfather. After five years as an apprentice a person with real aptitude will be hired by a master

13
00:01:16.019 --> 00:01:18.440
watchmaker to be an assistant in his workshop.

14
00:01:19.321 --> 00:01:24.824
The work is tedious at times, the hours are long, and the pay is meager, but you learn

15
00:01:24.864 --> 00:01:25.164
a lot.

16
00:01:26.345 --> 00:01:32.740
After my apprenticeship I was fortunate to be hired by Monsieur Girard, a master watchmaker and one of

17
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.081
the best in Geneva.

18
00:01:35.882 --> 00:01:42.426
The rules of a watchmaker's education are set down by the Watchmakers' Guild of Geneva, which was established

19
00:01:42.526 --> 00:01:43.706
in 1601.

20
00:01:44.887 --> 00:01:50.410
The guild is a group that was created by talented watchmakers to ensure that every watchmaker in the

21
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.631
city is an artist of the highest quality.

22
00:01:54.212 --> 00:01:59.175
In order to join the guild, a young man must work with a master watchmaker for three years,

23
00:01:59.995 --> 00:02:03.497
and within that time, he must create a watch to present to the guild.

24
00:02:04.598 --> 00:02:07.059
This watch is known as his masterpiece.

25
00:02:08.359 --> 00:02:13.521
If the guild approves his watch, the young man becomes a master and a member of the guild.

26
00:02:14.462 --> 00:02:16.963
Only then is he allowed to open his own shop.

27
00:02:18.184 --> 00:02:23.806
If the young watchmaker's watch is not approved, he can never open his own business and must toil

28
00:02:23.866 --> 00:02:26.127
as a watchmaker's assistant forevermore.

29
00:02:27.228 --> 00:02:32.261
As for me I successfully produced my masterpiece in just over a year.

30
00:02:33.542 --> 00:02:38.223
Naturally that watch is on display at my own workshop here on Saint-Gervais Street.

31
00:02:39.763 --> 00:02:42.744
Watchmaking requires many tools and accessories.

32
00:02:43.385 --> 00:02:52.027
First of all, there's my workbench, which is very important because it holds small hammers, brushes, tweezers, and

33
00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:54.648
anything else I need to repair or make a watch.

34
00:02:55.784 --> 00:03:02.347
This bench keeps my tools organized in small drawers, and I, like other watchmakers, have placed it in

35
00:03:02.387 --> 00:03:05.488
front of a large window that gets good natural light.

36
00:03:06.929 --> 00:03:13.411
Second, a watchmaker needs a magnifying eyeglass called a loupe to see close up the details of the

37
00:03:13.491 --> 00:03:14.352
inside of a watch.

38
00:03:15.692 --> 00:03:22.875
Third, various types of polishing and shaping implements are needed, such as oilstones and bluestones.

39
00:03:24.176 --> 00:03:29.181
Lastly, every watchmaker needs a small lathe, a very ancient tool.

40
00:03:30.121 --> 00:03:36.243
The lathe is a machine that holds a piece of material, such as wood or metal, in place

41
00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:37.464
and rotates it.

42
00:03:38.444 --> 00:03:43.185
This allows a craftsman to shape the material using both hands, as he likes.

43
00:03:44.866 --> 00:03:50.867
I have operated my own shop for almost twenty years, and currently I have two apprentices, Max and

44
00:03:50.927 --> 00:03:53.128
Julian, who help keep the workshop clean.

45
00:03:54.284 --> 00:04:00.748
We do a lot of filing and shaping of metal, which creates countless tiny slivers of brass and

46
00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:02.849
steel that get all over the place.

47
00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:09.852
We also use large amounts of sperm whale oil to make sure the watch parts move together smoothly,

48
00:04:10.513 --> 00:04:12.854
but it drips on everything and makes a mess.

49
00:04:14.155 --> 00:04:17.496
Watchmaking has changed substantially since my grandfather's day.

50
00:04:18.657 --> 00:04:21.799
Back then a fusee was commonly used in watches.

51
00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:27.882
A fusee is a kind of cone-shaped device that has a post attached to a wheel.

52
00:04:28.923 --> 00:04:34.446
A chain is wrapped around the fusee, and the chain is attached to a metal coil, which is

53
00:04:34.506 --> 00:04:37.768
around another post inside a barrel-shaped container.

54
00:04:38.668 --> 00:04:43.411
The metal coil turns the barrel, and then the barrel turns the fusee by pulling on the chain.

55
00:04:44.512 --> 00:04:49.054
The fusee moves the wheel, and time ticks by as the metal coil slowly unwraps.

56
00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:54.877
When the metal coil completely uncurls, it can be wound up again by a watch key.

57
00:04:55.737 --> 00:05:01.099
This older type of watch had only an hour hand, and it didn't keep time very well since

58
00:05:01.159 --> 00:05:02.579
it kept running down quickly.

59
00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:06.961
Plus these watches were also thick and heavy.

60
00:05:08.301 --> 00:05:14.724
People wore them on chains around their necks as jewelry, and the wealthy decorated them with precious gems

61
00:05:14.804 --> 00:05:15.464
and gold.

62
00:05:16.404 --> 00:05:22.788
But this custom changed when the preacher John Calvin came to Geneva in the mid-1500s and passed laws

63
00:05:23.208 --> 00:05:26.470
that forbade anyone from wearing big pieces of jewelry.

64
00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:33.714
The watchmakers quickly realized they'd have to make their watches smaller and lighter, and they also wanted them

65
00:05:33.754 --> 00:05:34.714
to keep better time.

66
00:05:35.675 --> 00:05:41.658
After many years of experimentation, last year the spiral balance spring was invented.

67
00:05:42.418 --> 00:05:47.617
It is a finely coiled spring made of steel that balances the movements of the watch.

68
00:05:48.778 --> 00:05:54.281
Imagine a grandfather clock with a pendulum moving back and forth at an even speed.

69
00:05:55.181 --> 00:05:57.763
This movement drives the ticking of the clock.

70
00:05:58.423 --> 00:06:02.505
This new little spring does the same thing inside a watch.

71
00:06:04.586 --> 00:06:12.210
Timekeeping has now become more accurate, and watches made now have two hands instead of one—another hand has

72
00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:13.671
been added to count the minutes.

73
00:06:14.372 --> 00:06:18.798
Just think how our concept of time has changed because of this little invention.

74
00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:24.102
Now I can tell precisely how late my apprentices are arriving at work in the morning!

75
00:06:25.363 --> 00:06:30.966
It has certainly been an exciting year for watchmakers; my workshop has a large number of orders for

76
00:06:31.027 --> 00:06:33.168
watches with the spiral balance spring.

77
00:06:34.109 --> 00:06:38.331
We will be quite busy, I can assure you, but we won't just be making watches.

78
00:06:39.312 --> 00:06:43.495
My shop also makes surgical instruments and mechanical figures called automata.

79
00:06:44.135 --> 00:06:49.499
But I must admit, for me, making a beautiful pocket watch is the most rewarding work of all.

80
00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:55.384
Even though I am now 41 years old and my eyesight is not as good as it once

81
00:06:55.464 --> 00:06:59.727
was, I can still make masterpieces that will last a long time.

82
00:07:00.948 --> 00:07:07.973
Combining beauty and quality is the key to making a good timepiece, and Jean-Paul Baptiste has always been

83
00:07:08.013 --> 00:07:10.475
known on Saint-Gervais Street as a true
